With the Mac driver option FitBelowNotch, a full-screen game uses only the area below the camera housing and the strip beside it is black; off by default.

This repository's patch to the Mac driver (version 2, after its adversarial review), mavrik's own; the route is VLC's
(2022: a black window over the whole screen, the picture below the safe area, as QuickTime Player does). Rebased
after 0034, whose full-screen pass shows the black window, and 0039.

Why: a full-screen game covers the whole panel, so on a MacBook with a camera housing its top 37 points (74 pixels
in Retina mode) are drawn behind the housing, which hides their middle (Civ VI's resource bar). macOS's own fix
(NSPrefersDisplaySafeAreaCompatibilityMode, Finder's "Scale to fit below built-in camera") is read from an app
bundle, which Wine's loader is not, changes the whole display on each activation and scales with black bars at the
sides too; Windows has no safe-area call a game could read. So the display itself is reported smaller.

- The option: HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver\FitBelowNotch=y, read prefix-wide like RetinaMode (the monitors must be
  the same in every process); off when absent. Off, or on a screen without a housing (an external display, a closed
  lid), nothing changes: every helper below then equals the code it replaces.
- The strip: the panel's own size below the camera, the tallest mode CoreGraphics lists at the screen's point width
  and a smaller height (1512x945 on a 14-inch MacBook Pro at its default 1512x982, so 37 points, and 3024x1890 in
  Retina mode), else the 16:10 size below it; never less than NSScreen's safeAreaInsets.top (32 there, Apple's line
  for "not hidden by the housing"), nor more than twice it. The screen's frame without it is the frame Windows sees.
  Each screen's strip is read on the main thread when the Cocoa app starts and when the screens change, and kept:
  the Unix side reads the kept values, never AppKit, and every process has Windows' y=0 from its start.
- What the game sees: that frame as the monitor (and the visible frame inside it as the work area), the desktop rect
  likewise; the current mode is the monitor's size (after Retina's doubling); the display offers that one mode, the
  current one with its position, with no synthesized 8 and 16 bpp copies, so win32u builds its standard sizes up to
  it and scales a smaller one into it uniformly, centred, with black bars (its own virtual modes, as with
  EmulateModeset); ChangeDisplaySettings then succeeds without changing the Mac's real mode, which would bring the
  whole panel back.
- Coordinates: Windows' y=0 moves to the bottom of the primary screen's strip (notch_offset). New helpers,
  cg{rect,point}_win_from_screen and cg{rect,point}_screen_from_win, add the offset to Retina's scale, and replace
  the old ones only where a rect or point is an absolute screen position: the monitors and the desktop rect; mouse
  moves, clicks, wheel turns, GetCursorPos, SetCursorPos and ClipCursor; the event queue's mouse positions after a
  warp; status item clicks; window frames to and from Wine (creation, moves, frame changes, live-resize queries,
  restore requests); the IME rect. Sizes and positions inside a window keep the old ones. flipRect and
  primaryScreenHeight keep the real frame: they also convert real Quartz positions.
- The pointer over the strip is at the game's top edge, as at a real screen's top: every absolute position sent to
  Windows (moves, clicks, wheel turns, GetCursorPos, the positions reset after a warp) is clamped to its screen's
  Windows frame, and the pinned-pointer test uses the Windows frames, so a move up there goes as a relative move
  with its true delta (a game's mouse-look keeps turning) and a move down there goes without its downward part
  until the pointer leaves the strip (sending it would be undone by a jump up as the pointer left).
- Full screen: a window covering a screen's Windows frame is full screen (screen_covered_by_rect); a scaled mode's
  window is too, since win32u frames a full-screen window at the whole raw monitor. It gets the full-screen level,
  0034 hides the Dock and menu bar, and constrainFrameRect leaves it alone. ClipCursor's "covers every screen means
  no clip" test uses the Windows frame too, so a full-screen clip stays no confinement, as today.
- A scaled mode's black bars: win32u passes the picture's rect (absolute, in Windows pixels) for the bars, which
  setMask: drew as points from the window's corner, twice the size in Retina mode, hiding the picture's top; it is
  now made relative to the window and converted to points. Without the option almost nothing reaches it (real mode
  lists keep win32u's scaled modes off), and on the main display with Retina mode off the rect is unchanged.
- The black strip: while Wine is active, each notched screen with a full-screen window of ours gets a black
  borderless window over its whole frame one level below the game (NSStatusWindowLevel: above the menu bar and the
  Dock), transient in Mission Control, never key or main. It takes clicks in the strip, so they reach neither the
  game nor the desktop; pointer moves over it go to the game window. It goes when Wine resigns active or no
  full-screen window is left, and follows the screens when they change (a new frame; a removed screen's is hidden).
- Screens changing (a lid closed, another resolution, a display unplugged) read the strips again before the change
  goes to Windows. With an external display above the built-in one, the strip is a gap between the two monitors in
  Windows' desktop, which the pointer crosses at the clamped edge.

MAVRIK_NOTCH_TRACE=1 prints each notched display's strip, how it was found, and its monitor on stderr.

Checked (mavrik S217 lab): applies after patches 0001 to 0039 with no fuzz; winemac builds with the engine's toolchain
with no new warning. It draws, so its probe (mavrik's tools/notchprobe.c: modes, monitor, metrics, a red bar on a
full-screen window's top rows, a cursor round trip, raw input and GetCursorPos with the pointer over the strip, a
1280x720 mode captured as a letterbox) runs in mavrik's away block, and the look in its walk.

